add('Polity & Governance','01 · Parliamentary calendar','Budget Session: January opening, February Budget','Parliament’s Budget Session commenced on 28 January.','The President addressed both Houses on 28 January.|The Economic Survey was tabled on 29 January.|The Union Budget date was 1 February, outside January.','Session scheduling, tabling a document and voting expenditure are different parliamentary actions.','Use actual dates rather than placing every event in the month of a review.|Future phases and scheduled sittings in a January snapshot are not proof they happened unchanged.','Do not backdate the February Budget to January or treat the Survey as an appropriation law.','How does the budget process combine executive planning and parliamentary scrutiny?',day=28)
add('Economy','01 · Survey versus estimate','Economic Survey: next-year projection is not an outturn','The 2025–26 Survey was tabled on 29 January.','It projects FY2026–27 real GDP growth at 6.8%–7.2%.|It identifies domestic demand and healthier balance sheets as supporting conditions.|It discusses external uncertainty and FDI below potential.','A policy assessment, statistical estimate and future growth forecast have different evidentiary roles.','Read the range with assumptions rather than as a guaranteed exact outcome.|Do not confuse this next-year range with January’s 7.4% first advance estimate for FY2025–26.','The Survey does not guarantee FY2026–27 growth or authorise every suggested policy.','What makes a growth outlook credible under global uncertainty?',day=29)
add('Economy','02 · National-account vintages','First advance GDP estimate: 7.4% for FY2025–26','The January first advance estimate covers the still-running financial year.','Real GDP is estimated to grow 7.4%.|GVA growth is estimated at 7.3%.|Manufacturing/construction are each estimated at 7%; agriculture at 3.1%.','Advance estimates extrapolate partial information and are revised; GDP differs from GVA.','Keep the then-used 2011–12 base and release vintage explicit.|Later rebasing and revised estimates should be added as dated updates, not silently substituted.','This is not a final full-year outcome or the same as December’s 7.3% RBI forecast.','How should policymakers use advance estimates without overstating precision?')
add('Economy','03 · Inflation and reference quarter','Q3 inflation: very low CPI, negative food and WPI','January’s review covers October–December 2025 prices.','Quarterly CPI inflation averaged 0.76%.|Food inflation averaged −3.88%.|WPI inflation averaged −0.17%.','Different baskets and coverage permit CPI and WPI inflation to have opposite signs.','A lower inflation rate is not the same as every price returning to an earlier level.|Distinguish these old-series quarterly figures from the later CPI-2024-base January release.','Do not label all figures January monthly inflation or infer every household experienced the same price change.','What does divergence between consumer and wholesale inflation reveal?')
add('Economy','04 · Fiscal composition','Survey fiscal lens: capital support and state rigidity','The Survey discusses fiscal consolidation and expenditure quality.','It expects the Centre’s FY2025–26 fiscal deficit below 4.5% of GDP.|Effective capital expenditure includes grants for creation of capital assets.|State cash transfers and committed spending affect budget flexibility.','Revenue expenditure classification does not alone determine social value; effective capex differs from only direct capital outlay.','Evaluate outcomes and financing sustainability rather than call all transfers wasteful or all capex productive.|AP fiscal analysis needs state-specific accounts, guarantees and payment obligations.','A Survey expectation is not an audited final deficit or proof every state has the same fiscal position.','How should fiscal consolidation protect valuable public services and investment?',True)
add('International Relations','01 · EU negotiation milestone','India–EU FTA: conclusion, not immediate universal free trade','Negotiations were concluded on 27 January.','The primary announcement describes preferential access for over 99% of Indian exports by trade value.|Tariff reductions include entry-into-force and schedule conditions.|Sensitive agricultural/dairy interests are safeguarded in the account.','Tariff-line counts, trade-value coverage, negotiation conclusion and legal entry into force differ.','AP marine, textile and food exporters also need origin, sanitary and traceability compliance.|Read EFTA, UK, Oman and NZ notes as separate agreements with different stages.','Do not call every product instantly duty-free or replace trade-value coverage with tariff-line share.','What determines whether negotiated market access becomes usable export opportunity?',True,src=['E8-EU','E8-PRS01'],day=27)
add('International Relations','02 · Services and carbon standards','EU services and CBAM: access does not erase regulation','The announcement includes services and carbon-related cooperation.','It describes EU commitments in 144 services subsectors.|Mobility is a facilitative framework, not unrestricted immigration.|CBAM language concerns engagement, recognition and support.','Tariff preferences do not automatically waive professional licensing, visas or carbon-border compliance.','Exporter readiness requires reliable emissions measurement and recognised verification.|Do not call cooperation a complete CBAM exemption without treaty/regulatory text establishing it.','A services commitment is not automatic practice rights for every profession in every member state.','How should services access and carbon compliance shape India’s trade strategy?',src=['E8-EU','E8-PRS01'])
add('International Relations','03 · UAE energy and technology','UAE visit: future LNG delivery and letters of intent','The UAE President visited on 19 January.','Agreements include LNG supply to HPCL starting in 2028 for ten years.|Defence, investment-zone and space cooperation include letters of intent.|Supercomputing and civil-nuclear cooperation were announced.','A commercial agreement, letter of intent and commissioned asset have different certainty and delivery stages.','Diversification can improve resilience but entails price, execution and infrastructure risks.|A 2032 trade target is not observed January trade.','Do not say 2028 LNG deliveries or every announced technology facility were already operating.','How can energy and technology partnerships support long-term resilience?',p='P2')
add('International Relations','04 · Germany partnership','Germany visit: declarations, institutions and transit limits','Chancellor Friedrich Merz visited on 12–13 January.','Cooperation covers defence industry, semiconductors and critical minerals.|Institutional MoUs also concern energy, IT and health.|A foreign-policy/security dialogue and transit facilitation were announced.','Airport transit permission is distinct from unrestricted entry, employment or permanent residence.','Technology partnerships require investment, standards and skills to yield durable gains.|Announcements need later operative instruments and project milestones.','Do not turn visa-free transit into unrestricted visa-free travel throughout Europe.','What converts strategic cooperation into usable industrial capability?',p='P2')
add('Culture & History','01 · Design protection proposal','Designs concept note: virtual interfaces and IP overlap','DPIIT consulted on possible amendments.','The note proposes coverage for virtual designs such as interfaces/icons.|It discusses the design–copyright overlap.|Deferred publication and international-registration options are proposed.','Industrial design protects visual appearance; it is not identical to a patent for technical function or every form of copyright.','Digital products need clarity without overbroad monopolies over basic interfaces.|Consultation about treaty accession is not proof all accessions or domestic amendments are complete.','Do not call a concept note enacted law or virtual-design proposals already universal rights.','How should design protection adapt to digital goods while preserving competition?',p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Equity regulations and judicial stay','UGC equity rules: notification followed by implementation stay','The Court stayed the 2026 regulations in the reviewed January position.','The regulations envisage Equal Opportunity Centres and Equity Committees.|The Court identified ambiguities and potential misuse concerns.|The review says the 2012 regulations continue during the stay.','An interim stay is not final invalidation; a notified framework may be temporarily inoperative.','Institutional inclusion needs clear definitions and fair accessible procedures.|Track later court orders rather than present every proposed 2026 deadline as operative.','Do not describe the 2026 framework as unconditionally in force or the stay as abolishing all anti-discrimination duties.','How can equity safeguards be both effective and procedurally fair?')
add('Society & Welfare','02 · Student wellbeing systems','UGC mental-health guidance: support and crisis readiness','UGC circulated a uniform policy framework for HEIs.','It envisages support centres and monitoring committees.|Helpline links with Tele-MANAS and district/national programmes feature.|Risk assessment, suicide prevention and post-crisis support are included.','Screening/support is not a substitute for clinical diagnosis and appropriate referral.','Confidentiality, trained staffing and continuity determine access beyond announcing a centre.|Do not treat all sensitive student data as routinely publishable performance material.','Guidance is not proof every campus has staffed services or a guarantee crises cannot occur.','What makes student wellbeing a functioning institutional duty rather than a helpline poster?',True)
add('Agriculture','01 · Pesticide life-cycle regulation','Draft Pesticide Management Bill: registration and licensing differ','The ministry sought comments on a replacement framework.','It covers manufacture, import, sale, use and disposal.|A Central Pesticides Board and Registration Committee are proposed.|Pesticide-product registration and activity licences have different functions.','Seed regulation, plant-variety rights and pesticide safety govern different agricultural risks.','Transparent reasons, testing and poisoning protocols matter alongside ease of registration.|AP crop protection needs effective extension, safer handling and functioning remedies.','A draft is not an enacted replacement of the 1968 Act or proof every registered pesticide is safe in every use.','How can pesticide regulation balance efficacy, safety and farmer access?',True)
add('Economy','05 · Multi-mineral concessions','Mineral Concession draft: adding minerals is not automatic','Coal-related draft rules address inclusion of additional minerals.','Lease holders must apply under proposed procedures.|Coal/lignite inclusion requires geological/mining-plan safeguards.|Discovery in a minor-mineral lease can trigger distinct consultation and allocation rules.','A right to mine one mineral does not automatically authorise extraction of every subsequently discovered mineral.','Transparent valuation and Centre/state coordination prevent windfalls and disputes.|Check final text and lease conditions before applying proposals to AP mineral areas.','Do not present a discovery as an unrestricted automatic right or all proposed decision periods as final law.','How should mineral discoveries within existing leases be governed?',p='P2')
add('Economy','06 · Mining amendment consultation','MMDR draft: concession ceilings and operationalisation','A statutory amendment draft was released for consultation.','It proposes changes to mineral-concession area limits.|Prospecting periods and central exploration-licence auction powers are addressed.|It proposes removal of a state extension power for delayed operationalisation.','Draft primary legislation differs from subordinate auction-rule consultation and actual grant of a lease.','Faster allocation requires credible exploration, environmental clearance and community safeguards.|Do not merge December coal proposals and every January mineral proposal into one already-enacted package.','Removing an area ceiling in a draft does not waive all mining/forest permissions.','How should mineral development improve speed without sacrificing accountability?')
add('Environment','01 · Forest and non-forest lease parts','Draft auction rules: partial execution is not forest clearance','The ministry proposed separate treatment of specified block portions.','Specified edge/low-resource forest portions may be excluded in the proposal.|Part-lease execution has mineral-type conditions.|Remaining areas need permits and no-objection clearances before the later stage.','Excluding land from a lease and obtaining legal clearance to work forest land are different actions.','Assess geological viability and impacts rather than assume segmentation eliminates cumulative risk.|Check upfront-payment conditions and final legal text.','Do not say the proposal authorises mining unapproved forest areas or applies identically to every deep-seated mineral block.','Can staged leasing accelerate development while preserving meaningful safeguards?',p='P2')
add('Environment','02 · Penalties and remediation finance','Environment Protection Fund: earmarking needs accountable use','The government notified fund rules.','Penalties under the specified Air, Water and Environment Acts flow to the fund.|Uses include monitoring, laboratories, remediation and capacity building.|Administrative expenses are capped at 5% in the reviewed account.','A collected penalty and an actual remediation project are separate financial/administrative stages.','Transparent allocations and outcome reporting should link pollution harm with restoration.|Penalty receipts should not become an incentive to tolerate preventable damage.','Do not call the fund a universal compensation payment to every affected resident.','How should environmental penalty revenues produce measurable restoration?')
add('Environment','03 · Solid waste transition','SWM Rules 2026: January notification, April commencement','The review records replacement rules notified in January.','The stated effective date is 1 April 2026.|Applicability extends to rural local bodies.|Responsibilities span generators, local bodies and other entities.','Notification and commencement differ; a rulebook does not create collection/processing capacity by itself.','AP municipalities and panchayats need coordinated budgets, segregation and treatment systems.|The Gazette fetch failed in this pass; this note relies on the substantive PRS account, not an independently read primary rulebook.','Do not backdate full operation to January or describe the new framework as urban-only.','How can waste regulation turn formal duties into reliable local service?',True)
add('Environment','04 · Bulk generators and residue','Waste duties: expanded generator criteria and local processing','The new rules expand the covered bulk-generator definition.','The review includes floor area of at least 20,000 square metres as a criterion.|Water use of at least 40,000 litres a day is another criterion.|Residential societies and decentralised wet-waste processing are addressed.','Alternative coverage thresholds must not be read as cumulative conditions all needing to be met together.','Local enforcement should help generators comply with segregation and processing, not only register them.|Agri/horticultural residue storage and prevention of open burning require practical facilities.','Do not assume an entity escapes coverage solely because it falls below one of several alternative thresholds.','What makes bulk-generator obligations administratively fair and environmentally useful?',True)
add('Environment','05 · Consent and compliance','Industrial consent amendments: longer validity is not immunity','Uniform consent guidelines under Air/Water Acts were amended.','The reviewed Consent to Operate lasts until cancelled.|Registered environmental auditors may verify compliance.|Specified estate-based micro/small enterprises have a self-certified establishment-consent route.','Consent to Establish, Consent to Operate and environmental clearance are distinct instruments.','Reduced renewal friction needs credible risk-based verification and cancellation powers.|Do not assume all plants receive deemed consent or external auditing eliminates Board responsibility.','Continuing validity is not permission to pollute without conditions or immunity from inspection.','How can consent reform lower friction without weakening enforcement?',True)
add('Environment','06 · Industrial intensity expansion','Carbon compliance: four additional industries','The government extended the GHG-intensity framework.','Additional industries include secondary aluminium and petroleum refineries.|Petrochemicals and textiles are also included.|The rules support compliance under the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme.','Intensity-based targets measure emissions per unit of output; absolute emissions can still rise as output grows.','Connect October’s initial sectors to January’s extension without treating either as an economy-wide cap.|Reliable baselines and accredited verification underpin credible credits.','Do not equate intensity reduction with an identical absolute reduction or voluntary offset purchasing.','How can expanding industrial carbon compliance preserve measurement credibility?')
add('Environment','07 · Ash proposal','Fly-ash draft: proposed thresholds and penalties','The ministry invited comments on ash-utilisation changes.','The proposal changes institutional planning roles.|It removes a specified 25% ash-mixing threshold.|It proposes reducing a user-agency fine from ₹1,500 to ₹1,000 per tonne.','Changing a prescribed mix ratio is not abolishing every utilisation or safety duty.','Mine suitability, transport and leachate/dust risks still require evidence.|Use final notification before applying a consultation-stage fine to liability.','Do not call proposed lower fines already operative or all ash automatically harmless.','How can ash reuse policy prevent risk shifting to mines and communities?',True,p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','01 · Research versus market approval','Drug-rule amendments: simplified research routes with exceptions','The 2019 New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules were amended.','Specified non-commercial manufacture moves to online intimation.|Certain bioavailability/bioequivalence studies have an intimation route.|Specified high-risk drug test licences retain permission, with a shorter timeline.','Research manufacture, study permission and commercial marketing approval are different regulatory stages.','Proportionate facilitation must retain ethics, quality and participant-safety duties.|Do not generalise a narrow waiver to every clinical trial or high-risk product.','Online intimation is not automatic proof a new drug can be sold to patients.','How can research regulation reduce delay while protecting safety?')
add('Economy','07 · Urban cooperative banking','UCB licensing discussion: consultation is not reopening','RBI sought views on resuming new UCB licensing.','The paper weighs local inclusion against capital/governance constraints.|It proposes eligibility conditions for established credit societies.|Capital, track record and asset-quality requirements feature.','One-member-one-vote ownership and banking prudential standards address different governance objectives.','Umbrella technology support can help but does not replace competent boards and supervision.|Use the dated paper, not assume every credit society acquired a banking licence.','Do not describe discussion proposals as final licences or apply a capital threshold as a deposit guarantee.','What conditions make cooperative banking expansion prudent and inclusive?',True)
add('Society & Welfare','03 · Contributory pensions','APY continuation: pension starts at sixty, depends on contributions','The Cabinet extended support through FY2030–31.','APY offers a guaranteed minimum ₹1,000–₹5,000 monthly pension from age sixty based on contributions.|Support covers outreach/development and viability gaps.|The scheme launched in May 2015, not January 2026.','A contributory pension differs from an immediate unconditional state cash transfer.','Coverage requires affordable regular contributions and accurate beneficiary information.|AP readers must distinguish national APY from Andhra Pradesh-specific pension schemes.','Do not promise every person ₹5,000 immediately or confuse APY’s acronym with an AP state entitlement.','How can contributory pensions reach workers with volatile informal incomes?',True,src=['E8-APY','E8-PRS01'],day=21)
add('Awards & Sports','01 · Athlete representation','Sports-body rules: merit, representation and elections','Rules were issued under the 2025 governance Act.','General bodies must include at least four sportspersons of outstanding merit in the reviewed framework.|Half of those athlete representatives must be women.|Eligibility, rosters, disqualification and election officers have specified conditions.','An athlete-representation minimum does not mean exactly four members or a 50% quota for every position in the whole federation.','Representation needs meaningful influence, fair selection and credible elections.|Follow July/August legislative stages into January rules rather than assume no later progress.','Do not generalise a rule for one membership category to every seat in every sports body.','How should sports governance combine autonomy, athlete voice and accountability?')
add('Awards & Sports','02 · Board selection procedures','Sports Board selection: committee versus appointed board','Separate rules specify the search-cum-selection mechanism.','The committee is headed by the Cabinet Secretary.|It prepares panels for appointments.|Panel validity and re-appointment conditions are specified.','A selection committee and the statutory board it helps appoint are different institutions.','Transparent criteria and conflict safeguards matter more than merely constituting a panel.|Rules publication does not prove every vacancy was filled or every body operational.','Do not confuse committee membership with board membership or the panel with a completed appointment.','What makes appointment design support regulatory independence?',p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','02 · Representation and federalism','Delimitation debate: no automatic redrawing on 1 January 2026','The magazine revisits southern-state representation concerns.','Articles 82 and 170 govern readjustment arrangements.|Parliamentary law and the relevant census/publication conditions matter.|Seat allocation and constituency-boundary changes are related but distinct.','A constitutional reference to the first census after 2026 is not a self-executing redistribution at the start of 2026.','Balance population equality, federal trust and incentives for demographic achievement.|Avoid treating “last done in 2002” as the implementation date for all boundaries or saying every delimitation order is absolutely beyond constitutional scrutiny.','Do not promise a known new seat count for AP before the legal process and official outcome.','How should representation reform reconcile democratic equality and federal legitimacy?',True,slug='delimitation')
add('Polity & Governance','03 · Anti-corruption safeguards','Section 17A split verdict: no single majority outcome yet','The magazine reports divergent judicial opinions.','The challenge concerns prior approval for specified decision-related corruption inquiries/investigation.|One opinion questions constitutionality; another supports a conditional approach.|A split differs from a binding unanimous invalidation.','Investigation approval under Section 17A differs from prosecution sanction; scope and statutory exceptions matter.','Protect honest decisions without creating an executive shield for corrupt conduct.|The primary judgment was not independently read here, so precise operative directions need checking.','Do not declare Section 17A finally struck down or finally upheld solely from a split bench.','How should anti-corruption law balance decision-making confidence with investigative independence?',slug='prevention-of-corruption-act-1988-1')
add('Society & Welfare','04 · Urban drinking-water safety','Urban water: a pipe connection is not safe service','An Indore contamination crisis prompted the magazine’s governance discussion.','The article reports NHRC scrutiny of municipal supply vulnerability.|Water quality, sanitation and network integrity interact.|State/local institutions hold major service responsibilities.','Supply infrastructure, continuous pressure, safe quality and accessible complaint handling are different service dimensions.','AP towns need surveillance, testing, pipe/sewer mapping and rapid alerts.|Do not infer a final lab-confirmed causal finding for every illness from one secondary account.','Connections alone do not certify potable water or every reported pathogen in every household sample.','How can urban water governance prevent rather than only respond to contamination?',True,slug='urban-water-governance')
add('Economy','08 · Audited state-finance period','State Finances 2023–24: publication is not current-year accounts','The magazine reviews CAG’s consolidated state-finance publication.','It covers audited FY2023–24 data and a ten-year trend.|Own taxes, devolution, grants and non-tax revenue have different roles.|Committed expenditure and contingent liabilities constrain flexibility.','Borrowing receipts are not recurrent revenue; a government guarantee is not identical to debt already called.','Use AP’s own accounts and disclosures before projecting aggregate findings onto the state.|A January publication cannot be treated as audited FY2025–26 expenditure.','Do not equate every state with the aggregate or call all guarantees already cash expenditure.','How should states protect fiscal flexibility while funding essential services?',True,slug='state-finances-1')
add('Economy','09 · Export preparedness framework','EPI 2024: composite readiness, published in 2026','NITI’s official page and report framework were inspected.','The business ecosystem pillar has 40% weight.|Export infrastructure, policy/governance and performance have 20% each.|Large states and smaller/NE states/UTs have category comparisons.','Composite readiness includes outcomes and enabling conditions; it is not simply a ranking of export value.','Interpret shifts with methodology changes, not as pure year-on-year performance changes.|Use district/value-chain bottlenecks to translate a score into action.','Do not confuse the 2024 report label with its January 2026 publication or readiness rank with export volume rank.','How can benchmarking improve policy without encouraging rank-chasing?',src=['E8-EPI'],slug='export-preparedness-index-epi')
add('Economy','10 · CPI rebasing and later evidence','CPI-2024 supplement: January reference, February publication','The January magazine includes later release information; the MoSPI FAQ corroborates methodology.','The new base is 2024=100 with HCES2023–24-derived weights.|Weighted items increase from 299 to 358.|The FAQ describes elementary Jevons and higher-level Young/modified-Laspeyres methods.','Index reference period, weight reference survey and publication date are distinct.','The FAQ’s 12 February release/back-series context must remain explicit; do not backdate the new release to January.|Classification changes mean old/new food-weight percentages need like-for-like comparisons.','January reference data is not evidence the series was published in January; rebasing alone does not prove inflation fell.','Why does credible inflation measurement require periodic rebasing and transparent linking?',src=['E8-CPI'],slug='revision-of-consumer-price-index')
add('Environment','08 · Glacial hazard assessment','GLOFs: lake expansion is only one part of risk','The magazine discusses growing glacial lakes and assessment gaps.','An outburst is sudden release from a glacier-associated lake.|Dam stability, mass movements and exposure shape consequences.|Early warning, mapping and community preparedness are complementary.','Hazard probability, exposed assets and vulnerability are different risk dimensions.','Prioritise dangerous lake/cascade systems rather than rank risk from lake count alone.|AP disaster planning shares vulnerability/communication principles, not identical glacial hazards.','Do not call every large glacial lake imminently unstable or treat every Himalayan flood as a proven GLOF.','How should mountain-risk governance connect monitoring, land use and warning systems?',slug='glacial-lake-outburst-floods-glofs-1')
add('Security','01 · Hypersonic technology stages','Scramjet: twelve-plus-minute ground test, not deployed missile','DRDL tested a full-scale actively cooled combustor on 9 January.','The primary release states a run time over twelve minutes.|It builds on the April2025 subscale ground test.|Scramjet combustion remains supersonic and uses atmospheric oxygen.','A ground combustor test differs from integrated flight testing, weapon qualification and operational deployment.','Cooling, ignition/flame stability and integration matter beyond a long run time.|Do not generalise one fuel choice to every scramjet or compare all flight regimes with a universal efficiency claim.','The test does not establish a newly deployed missile or a twelve-minute operational flight.','What engineering stages bridge a propulsion demonstration and reliable deployment?',src=['E8-SCRAM'],slug='scramjet-engine-2')
add('Culture & History','02 · Somnath commemoration','Somnath: location, living significance and reconstructed form','The magazine reviews Somnath Swabhiman Parv and the temple’s history.','Somnath is at Prabhas Patan near Veraval, Gujarat.|The present form is associated with Maru-Gurjara architecture.|The post-independence consecration took place in 1951.','A present reconstructed monument’s form differs from every phase of its earlier built history.','Separate devotional tradition, documented historical chronology and contemporary commemoration.|Do not repeat unsupported exact attack counts or attribute the origin of an entire style to one temple.','Do not relocate the temple or assume the present structure survives unchanged from ancient times.','How should heritage study combine cultural meaning with careful historical evidence?',slug='somnath-temple-2',p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','05 · GS4 synthetic-media harm','AI social-media ethics: consent, dignity and remedies','The issue discusses misuse of generative tools against women and children.','Synthetic sexualised imagery can violate dignity even when the depicted act never occurred.|Platform incentives, model safeguards and reporting systems affect risk.|Privacy, discrimination and accountability are distinct concerns.','Authenticity labels alone cannot establish consent, truth or lawful purpose.','Use accessible rapid reporting, preservation of relevant evidence and proportionate safeguards.|Protect complainants without assuming every allegation has already been legally adjudicated.','Do not treat fabricated content as harmless merely because it is artificial or a label as a complete remedy.','What responsibilities should platforms and AI developers bear for predictable misuse?',slug='ethical-concerns-with-ai-use-on-social-media',p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Export rank and readiness','AP EPI: fifth, 60.65, Leader category','The official EPI report ranking table was inspected.','AP’s score is 60.65.|It ranks fifth in the Large States table and overall table inspected.|It is classified as a Leader; this is EPI2024 published in January2026.','Preparedness scores combine infrastructure, ecosystem, governance and performance, not only export volume.','Read the score with method and data year before claiming a causal jump from one recent policy.|Ports, inland connectivity, quality and cold chains need district-level follow-through.','Do not call fifth place a rank in total export value or treat all districts as equally prepared.','How should Andhra Pradesh convert export readiness into diversified district-level opportunity?',True,src=['E8-EPI'])
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Logistics finance','APLINC and AP Logistics Fund: distinct vehicles','The 8 January Cabinet approved the institutional proposals.','APLINC is described as an integrated infrastructure holding company.|The AP Logistics Fund is proposed as an AIF.|The state is the initial sponsor with up to 20% stake in the secondary account.','A holding company, pooled investment fund and project SPV serve different functions and carry different risks.','Institutional mobilisation needs governance, investor protections and credible project cash flows.|Cabinet approval does not prove SEBI registration, full fundraising or asset construction.','Do not call an AIF a universal government grant or announced sponsor share a completed contribution.','How can AP mobilise infrastructure capital while controlling contingent fiscal risk?',True,src=['E8-APCAB','E8-APHINDU'],day=8)
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Cluster common facilities','AP clusters: forty-five CFCs are not 175 finished parks','The Cabinet approved the Cluster Development Programme.','The secondary accounts give an outlay of ₹200 crore.|The target is forty-five MSME Common Facility Centres.|Shared facilities differ from the earlier constituency-park ambition.','A CFC provides shared services/infrastructure; a park is a broader spatial development model.','Match facilities to user demand, equipment maintenance and fair small-firm access.|Do not double-count this programme as forty-five wholly new operational industrial parks.','Approval is not completion, and CFCs are not identical to the November 175-park plan.','What makes common facilities raise MSME productivity rather than become underused assets?',True,src=['E8-APCAB','E8-APHINDU'],day=8)
add('Andhra Pradesh','04 · Kadapa manufacturing proposal','Kadapa electrical-steel and wind-mast facility: approval stage','The Cabinet account identifies Shirdi Sai Electricals’ proposal.','It concerns a greenfield facility in Kadapa district.|The proposed product areas are electrical steel and wind masts.|This is an investment approval, not verified commissioned output or jobs.','Electrical steel’s magnetic properties differ from a generic claim all steel grades are interchangeable.','Local value depends on project execution, skills, suppliers and environmental compliance.|Check official incentives/land/clearances and actual timelines before asserting delivery.','Do not invent employment totals, factory completion or a universal subsidy from the approval headline.','How can Kadapa build local industrial linkages from an approved investment?',True,src=['E8-APCAB'],day=8)
add('Andhra Pradesh','05 · Aerospace land allotment','Tirupati/Chittoor aerospace proposals: land before production','The Cabinet cleared specified land allotments in the secondary account.','About 150 acres concern a rocket/engine unit in Tirupati’s proposed Space City.|About 56 acres concern a Hansa-3(NG) trainer/manufacturing and training proposal in Chittoor.|Different firms/projects have separate approvals.','Industrial land allotment does not establish a rocket launch licence or certified aircraft production.','Supply chains and skilled employment require milestones, capital and regulatory readiness.|Do not relocate the national launch site or treat the industrial proposal as an already operational spaceport.','A manufacturing proposal is not a successful launch or a completed flight-training operation.','What complementary institutions make aerospace clusters commercially credible?',True,src=['E8-APCAB'],day=8)
add('Andhra Pradesh','06 · Water-finance guarantee','AP water loan guarantee: contingent liability, not grant receipt','The Cabinet approved a guarantee supporting water infrastructure finance.','The reported amount is a ₹5,000 crore NaBFID loan.|The recipient is AP Jal Jeevan Water Supply Corporation.|A guarantee supports borrowing; drawdown and repayment have separate stages.','A guaranteed loan is not an unconditional Central grant and may create contingent state exposure.','Water-service outcomes require project appraisal, procurement, operations and safe supply.|Report guarantees and realised borrowings transparently instead of counting the full sanction as completed capital expenditure.','Do not call the amount already spent or every household provided safe water from approval alone.','How should public-service borrowing balance investment needs and fiscal transparency?',True,src=['E8-APCAB'],day=8)
add('Andhra Pradesh','07 · Pumped storage stages','Kamalapadu pumped storage: DPR sanction, not new generation delivered','The Cabinet approved the project DPR and administrative sanction.','The proposal is for 950 MW in Anantapur district.|The secondary report gives estimated investment of ₹7,377 crore.|Construction, clearances and operation remain distinct stages.','Pumped storage consumes electricity to lift water and later recovers part of it; MW capacity is not MWh stored.','Assess round-trip efficiency, water, land, grid connections and dispatch economics.|Do not present storage as a net primary renewable-energy source by definition.','A DPR approval is not a commissioned 950-MW plant or proof a specific energy duration.','How can pumped storage support AP reliability while managing resource constraints?',True,src=['E8-APCAB'],day=8)
add('Science & Technology','02 · Launch anomaly evidence','PSLV-C62: Sriharikota launch and PS3 anomaly','Contemporary reporting records the 12 January mission anomaly.','The mission launched from Satish Dhawan Space Centre, Sriharikota.|ISRO-attributed reporting places the anomaly near the end of PS3.|A detailed analysis was initiated; this source does not establish final root cause.','Successful lift-off, reaching orbit, mission success and a final failure finding are different milestones.','Use dated investigative evidence rather than extrapolate cause from the stage where a disturbance appeared.|Payload counts/descriptions that differ across reports are not fixed here without primary reconciliation.','Do not call the mission wholly successful from lift-off or invent a final engineering cause.','How should space-programme accountability communicate failure without premature attribution?',True,src=['E8-PSLV'],day=12)
